From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Randolph <noreply@platform.uno>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: classify GC handles rooting condemned collectible ALCs

A downstream WASM host doing repeated collectible-ALC load/unload cycles
still strands one context per cycle with the forced-unload opt-in
active: the teardown reports NotQuiescent indefinitely, so some root
keeps the condemned contexts' LoaderAllocator witnesses alive. An
exhaustive managed-side investigation (a BFS root tracer over every
non-collectible static, ConditionalWeakTable enumeration, and direct
probes of the interop/RPC tables) found no managed root -- which leaves
exactly the roots managed reflection CANNOT enumerate: raw GCHandles,
thread stacks/registers, and native structures.

This patch adds read-only diagnostics that narrow that down from inside
the runtime, through ONE new icall that returns the whole measurement as
a single tuple:

  * sgen_gchandle_foreach_slot_bounded (sgen-gchandles.c) is a new
    read-only, RAW-SLOT-BOUNDED walk over one handle table. It exists
    because sgen_gchandle_iterate cannot serve a synchronous diagnostic:
    that iterator always walks a table to its persistent next_slot
    high-water mark -- which sgen_gchandle_free never lowers, since
    freeing only NULLs a slot and refills reuse holes -- and its
    callback cannot stop it. The new entry point clamps the raw slot
    range, reports the mark it was eligible to cover, and writes no slot
    at all (its callback returns nothing), so there is no self-store and
    no dislink protocol update either.
  * sgen_scan_handles_into_managers (sgen-mono.c) drives that walk and
    reports, per handle, whether the handle's TARGET is attributable to
    the condemned set. ONE budget is spent across all four type passes,
    so total cost is a fixed number of slot loads regardless of the
    tables' history.
  * assembly-load-context.c builds the condemned set (every collectible
    + unloading context's primary manager plus each shared generic
    manager it owns) and exposes it through
    InternalGetCondemnedSnapshot, which fills a caller-supplied gint32
    buffer with ten fields: condemned managers in scope; how many of
    them still have a live LoaderAllocator witness; four handle buckets
    (strong/pinned/weak/weak-track) attributed BY THE TARGET'S CLASS;
    identity-attributed handles; raw slots visited; raw slots eligible;
    and a saturation flag.

ONE CALL, ONE TUPLE. The first revision of this surface exposed those
fields as fourteen additional integer "kinds" of the existing
InternalGetScoutRetirementStats, served out of a process-global latched
snapshot, with atomicity supplied by a caller-side
refresh / read-gauges / validate-token protocol plus a retry. Review
found three separate defects in that shape, all instances of the same
root cause -- consistency enforced by caller CONVENTION rather than by
the surface:

  * a mixed-generation read, needing only two LOGICAL callers and no
    native thread race: A refreshes and reads one gauge, yields, B
    refreshes, A reads the rest out of B's generation;
  * a token contract that documented a monotonic clock while the
    counter wrapped, so a "newer than" comparison rejected correct
    reads at the boundary;
  * a test-only seam that reseeded the free-running counter but left
    the LATCHED token untouched, so reseeding while the latch already
    held G_MAXINT32 republished a token a stale reader still accepted --
    re-arming exactly the tear the token existed to reject.

Filling the whole tuple in a single call removes that class of defect
rather than the individual instances. There is no latch, no
process-global mutable state for this surface, no generation token, no
retry loop, and no test-only stat kinds on the production dispatch
switch: the two that existed (a self-test cap and the counter seed)
are gone, the cap becoming an ordinary caller-chosen budget that the
runtime clamps. InternalGetScoutRetirementStats is left exactly as it
was, and unknown kinds keep returning -1.

The fill is ALL-OR-NOTHING: it returns the number of fields written,
0 when the caller's buffer cannot hold the whole tuple (writing
nothing, so a stale buffer stays stale rather than half-updated), and
-1 where the feature is compiled out. -1 therefore means UNAVAILABLE
and never "zero"; a live surface with an empty condemned set honestly
reports 0 in every field.

WHAT THIS PROVES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. Attribution is PER HANDLE and
ONE HOP. A non-zero bucket establishes that a GC handle DIRECTLY TARGETS
an object attributable to the condemned set -- a NECESSARY, not
sufficient, condition for "a GC handle is the holder". All-zero buckets
do NOT establish that no GC handle is involved, because rooting is
transitive: a handle whose immediate target is corelib-classed while its
object graph reaches a condemned instance lands in no bucket. The miss
classes are enumerated in the contract comment (containers such as
object[]/List<object>/Dictionary, the RuntimeType/RuntimeAssembly/
MethodInfo reflection-cache family, delegates, pinned interop buffers,
and anything reached only through native structures or thread
stacks/registers). The sibling regression
ForcedUnload_RootedPointerFreeObjects_BlockNativeFree already
demonstrates that BCL-typed roots block the free, i.e. exactly the shape
class attribution cannot see. These fields are a triage funnel, never a
proof of absence.

Identity attribution exists because of that limit: this very patch
series promotes the native->managed ALC handle to STRONG while an unload
is in flight, and the managed wrapper is corelib-classed, so class
attribution is blind to the runtime's OWN most important root. Identity
attribution catches it (and each manager's LoaderAllocator witness) and
counts it separately, so the runtime's expected roots stay
distinguishable from a third party's. It is class-blind but NOT
table-blind: like the class buckets it sees only handles of the four
scanned types, so its scope is "any of those four", never "any handle in
the runtime".

SINGLE-SOURCED CONTRACT. The "Root classification for condemned
collectible contexts" block in assembly-load-context.c is the one place
the field catalog, the attribution limits, the buffer protocol and the
budget are defined. gc-internals.h, the managed bridge and the
regressions reference it instead of restating it; an earlier revision
restated the catalog in three places and the copies drifted.

REMOVAL. This is temporary scaffolding and ships with a tracking marker
saying so: a TODO in that same block names the tracking issue and both
removal conditions (the residual root is found and fixed, or the
stop-rule fires and the approach is abandoned rather than extended).
alc_is_condemned and alc_foreach_teardown_manager stay either way --
they are the single definition of "the managers a teardown would free"
and the quiescence gate itself consumes them, which is why the gate can
no longer disagree with the gauge that reports the quantity it refuses
on. The shared walk is also NULL-safe on both manager arrays, which the
hand-copied loops were not: the forced teardown NULLs them, so a
read-only gauge racing a tombstoned context could abort the runtime.

COST is O(managers + budget). Membership is a pointer-keyed set, so the
walk does not degrade quadratically as contexts strand (the state it
exists to explain), and it short-circuits entirely on an empty condemned
set. The handle walk is bounded on RAW SLOTS rather than on classified
hits, which is the only bound worth stating: a cap on hits would still
sweep a long, mostly-holey table end to end, once per type pass, while
every bucket read 0. The visited/eligible pair publishes what the walk
visited versus what it was eligible to visit, so the bound is observable
rather than merely asserted. The caller's budget is clamped to the
runtime's own maximum, so the pause bound stays a property of the
surface and not of whoever called it.

GATING -- the whole region is DISABLE_THREADS-only, and the sgen entry
points' declarations AND definitions are gated on DISABLE_THREADS as
well as HAVE_SGEN_GC, so the walk cannot be reached on a build where the
single-threaded precondition does not hold. Because the bounded walk
writes nothing, it needs no MONO_PERMIT (need (sgen_world_stopped)) of
its own -- exclusive access comes from the absence of other threads
rather than from a collection pause. What remained unproven is ASSERTED
rather than argued in prose: the walk g_asserts that no collection is in
progress and that it is not re-entered.

PURITY -- m_class_get_mem_manager is a pure lookup (generic-instance
owner, array element-class recursion, or the class's image ALC with the
default ALC's manager as the dynamic-assembly fallback), so it cannot
lazily allocate a manager mid-walk; that is what makes "read-only" true
of the whole operation and not merely of the slots. Dereferencing a
target's vtable is safe for the same reason the forced teardown is: a
manager is only freed once its whole witness set is dead, so no live
object can outlive its own class's manager.

SCOPE: thread-stack/register and native-structure roots are NOT
attributed here, and are not eliminated by empty buckets either (see
above). Attributing a pinning stack slot needs collection-time
instrumentation of sgen's pin path, deliberately left to a follow-up.

Five lifecycle regressions drive the surface end to end.

CondemnedHandleClassification_ReportsHoldingHandleByType uses EXACT
deltas rather than "more than baseline":

  * each of the four buckets moves by exactly +1 for its own handle type
    and exactly +0 for the others, measured across intervals whose only
    content is the GCHandle.Alloc calls under test -- so the buckets are
    proven separated by handle type, not merely non-zero. The pinned and
    weak-track buckets are exercised with real Pinned /
    WeakTrackResurrection handles on a pointer-free instance, not
    asserted to stay at zero;
  * identity attribution is proven to count HANDLES, not distinct
    objects: a second strong handle onto the ALREADY-registered managed
    ALC wrapper moves it by exactly +1 and back by exactly -1 on free,
    while the class buckets stay flat. An implementation reporting the
    SIZE of the identity set satisfies "identity >= 2" but fails that
    pair;
  * NEGATIVE CONTROLS: a handle on a plain corelib object moves nothing,
    and -- documenting the blind spot in an assertion -- neither does a
    handle on an object[] that HOLDS a collectible instance;
  * the STRONG handle alone holds the witness: after every other handle
    is freed it still pins the teardown at NotQuiescent, and freeing it
    drains the bucket by exactly one and lets the teardown complete;
  * set-wide fields are compared with <=, not equality: the GC-driven
    scout completion can retire a stray an earlier test left pending,
    which would make an exact equality order-dependent.

CondemnedSnapshot_SingleCallTuple_CannotBeTornByACompetingCaller pins
atomicity where it is observable -- at the boundary. A tuple that has
been returned is a VALUE: a competing caller's snapshot, taken while the
condemned set really changes underneath both of them, must not
retroactively alter it, and the newer tuple must see the change so the
first assertion cannot pass vacuously. Every tuple is additionally
required to be INTERNALLY COHERENT -- visited <= eligible, live
witnesses <= managers in scope, and the saturation flag agreeing with
the visited/eligible pair in BOTH directions. Those are precisely the
cross-field invariants a mixed-generation read could violate and a
single-call fill cannot.

CondemnedSnapshot_BufferContract_IsAllOrNothing drives the fill's
length and availability contract, on buffers poisoned first so "the
runtime wrote this" and "the runtime left this alone" are
distinguishable (0 would not be: 0 is a legitimate value for every
count). An UNDERSIZED buffer must be refused with 0 and left completely
untouched; an OVERSIZED one must receive EXACTLY the tuple with its
tail still poisoned, so the fill honours the caller's length as a bound
rather than assuming it; a threads-enabled runtime must report -1 and
write nothing; and an absurd slot budget (int.MaxValue) must be clamped
rather than trusted, while a negative one means "use the default".

CondemnedHandleWalk_SaturationFlag_IsSetWhenTheRawBudgetIsCrossed
exercises the POSITIVE saturation branch, which every other assertion in
the suite requires to be 0: the flag must read 1 under the reduced cap,
and the truncated counts must not exceed the full-budget ones.

CondemnedHandleWalk_RawSlotBudget_StopsShortOfAHoleyTablesHighWaterMark
builds the shape the bound exists for -- several hundred simultaneously
live strong handles, all then freed, leaving a table whose persistent
high-water mark is far above the cap while nearly every slot is a hole
-- and requires the visited count to stop at the cap rather than at the
eligible mark. Its scope is stated in the test rather than overclaimed:
that count is the runtime's own report and the only channel through
which the bound is observable, so the test pins the REPORTED bound and
catches the shape this replaced (a budget that gated only per-slot
classification while the swept range stayed at the high-water mark). The
sweep itself rests on the loop's range expression, which is a review
matter, not a test one.

All five assert both dark configurations off PlatformDetection, never
off the call under test: deriving the expectation from the same call
would be self-confirming, since a regression to -1 everywhere would
satisfy both dark legs vacuously. A threads-enabled runtime has the
machinery compiled out and must report -1 while writing nothing; the
single-threaded build with the opt-in off keeps the surface live and
must honestly report an EMPTY condemned set (0 in every field), with a
small budget unable to manufacture truncation out of a walk that never
ran.
